## Known Limitations

The limitations below are known and tracked for this release. Each is logged individually in the project issue tracker — check there for current status before starting an implementation.

### Platform support

Ansible-based setup is supported on **Ubuntu** and **macOS**. Windows is supported through **WSL2 only** (see the [Windows quickstart guide](../../WINDOWS-QUICKSTART.md)). Native Windows and Red Hat Linux are not yet supported.

### Migrating complaint categories from the previous version

The earlier two-level complaint-category master has been replaced by the new multi-level complaint hierarchy. A validated [migration tool](../migration/servicedefs-to-complainthierarchy-migration.md) is available and is the recommended path, but conversion is **not automatic** — it must be run once per city and the result confirmed.

Two things to watch during migration:

- Complaints whose category has not been migrated will still open and display correctly, but will **fail to send notifications** on their next workflow action (assignment, resolution or escalation).
- If you are migrating several cities that share common category codes, dashboard tiles can attribute a category to the wrong city's department. The migration documentation includes a query to check whether your deployment is affected.

### Jurisdiction and department access control

This is a new capability. We recommend verifying it against your own role setup before enabling it in production — particularly if your city relies on tenant-wide admin or supervisor roles seeing complete data.

- Tenant-wide admin and supervisor roles can lose unrestricted dashboard access and see an empty dashboard.
- The **Department Grievance Routing Officer (DGRO)** role receives the Dashboard navigation link but not the permission to use it, so requests are denied. Grant DGRO the same analytics permissions as other supervisor-level roles if this role needs dashboard access.
- Filter dropdowns in complaint search and the employee inbox list every jurisdiction and department in the city, not only those the signed-in employee is scoped to. Scoping of the **results** is correct; only the picker's contents are wrong.
- A jurisdiction or department can be granted to an employee through the Configurator, but there is no corresponding way to remove one.
- Masking of personal information on the employee complaint timeline is not yet applied, even where visibility settings suggest otherwise.

See [jurisdiction-access-control.md](../jurisdiction-access-control.md) for how this feature works in detail.

### Supervisor Dashboard

- Some indicators are not yet available or return incorrect values, including SLA non-compliance rate, employees with most open complaints, employee performance, and complaints at risk.
- CSV export writes raw numbers in place of percentages for percentage-based indicators.
- The dashboard is not yet localised into Portuguese, and load behaviour at higher record volumes has not yet been benchmarked.

### Complaint workflow

- After a complaint escalates automatically, completing the workflow from the escalated assignee's side does not always work correctly.
- When assigning a complaint between grievance routing officers and last-mile resolvers, the assignee list does not always filter correctly by role.
- Last-mile resolvers can occasionally be unable to mark a complaint resolved. This is a caching issue rather than a workflow defect.

### Notifications

- If the notification provider's access token expires, SMS and WhatsApp messages **stop sending with no error surfaced in the Configurator**. Check the provider console before treating this as a configuration problem.
- Employees are not yet notified when a complaint is assigned to them, on any channel.
- Some template synchronisation and notification-configuration actions do not yet behave as expected.
- Setup documentation for implementation teams is still being published.

### Two-way WhatsApp engagement

Available as a **multi-city sandbox pilot**, not as a generally available feature.

### Other areas in progress

Observability dashboards for operations and maintenance, rate-limiter configuration and vulnerability testing remain open for this release. Citizen login on the Docker Compose setup, tenant-level validation of complaint types and department masters, and a dashboard data dictionary are also in progress.

